WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.701
-Our first guest is an
Emmy-winning

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:06.300
comedian and the host of
"The Sarah Silverman Podcast."

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.267
You can also see her
in the new film "Marry Me,"

00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:11.367
in theaters and streaming on
Peacock February 11th.

00:00:11.367 --> 00:00:13.634
Here is Sarah Silverman!

00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:15.400
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.968
Hi! Yeah!
-What's up?!

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:24.067
-Oh! Can you feel the love from
New York for Sarah Silverman?

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:25.234
-Hi!
-Hi, bud.

00:00:25.234 --> 00:00:27.467
-I brought you a gift.

00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:28.634
-Oh, you -- Thank you.

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:31.767
-Yeah, because it's close
to Valentine's Day,

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:34.634
and the movie "Marry Me"
comes out on Valentine's Day.

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:37.434
-Yep.
-And I know you like chocolate.

00:00:37.434 --> 00:00:39.901
And so I thought
I'd just give you this.

00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:43.734
But I'm not there, I realized.

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:44.868
-Yeah, I thought someone
was going to

00:00:44.868 --> 00:00:47.334
bring it out to me
or something, but no.

00:00:47.334 --> 00:00:48.367
Yeah.
-No.

00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:52.100
-No.
-I'll just, like, ASMR it.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.400
-[ Laughing ] Oh, my --
-Oh, my God!

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.934
-Oh, please.
Well, you know what it is?

00:00:55.934 --> 00:00:59.567
It's the thought --
-You would love this.

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:00.634
-You know what it is?

00:01:00.634 --> 00:01:01.968
It's the thought
that counts, Sarah.

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:04.100
How many chocolates
are in that box?

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.534
-I only ate one,
but then I was like,

00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:06.934
"I can't swallow it whole."

00:01:06.934 --> 00:01:08.200
-That's true, yeah.
-You have to savor it.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.033
-No, you have to savor it.
Sarah --

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:11.467
-Go on, go on. I'll be ready.

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:15.467
-You're back to doing stand-up,
which I'm excited about.

00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:18.000
What things are you
talking about now in your act?

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.400
-Can I just do a bit
that isn't even a bit?

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:21.968
-Yeah.
-I don't know what this is.

00:01:21.968 --> 00:01:24.501
-Okay. I love this.

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:30.367
-It's something like
if you went up to somebody,

00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:33.000
like, you walked up
to a stranger,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.968
and you could say
something like, "Hi.

00:01:37.968 --> 00:01:39.200
You don't know me.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.501
Oh, this is so weird.

00:01:40.501 --> 00:01:46.467
But I -- Somebody you love
who has passed away

00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:50.501
wants you to know
it's not your fault?"

00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:54.000
I mean...
-That's fantastic.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.267
-...I just think
it's interesting.

00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:57.734
-Wow! They're strangers.

00:01:57.734 --> 00:02:04.000
-Because, Jimmy, it's a lie,
but it's also, like, the truth,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.334
you know?

00:02:05.334 --> 00:02:09.601
There isn't anybody who
that wouldn't be very moving

00:02:09.601 --> 00:02:13.434
and, like,
release them from burdens.

00:02:13.434 --> 00:02:14.834
But it is a lie.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:16.000
Yeah.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.667
-You're alleviating people
from the guilt.

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:18.801
I think it's fantastic.

00:02:18.801 --> 00:02:20.501
And I think it's --
I think it's a good bit.

00:02:20.501 --> 00:02:26.200
Are you doing any joke,
like, specific joke-jokes?

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.801
-Ooh! I wrote, like,
jokey-joke,

00:02:28.801 --> 00:02:31.133
like a joke-book kind of joke.

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:32.133
-I love this.
I want to hear this.

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:34.067
-Alright.
-Yes, we love this.

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:37.000
-Okay.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.868
What did the Jewish mother

00:02:41.868 --> 00:02:45.067
say to her porn-star daughter

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:47.834
after watching her
in a gangbang?

00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:52.767
[ Drumroll ]

00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:55.767
-What?
-"You were the best one."

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:02.033
-Oh, my goodness.
-"You were the best one."

00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:04.901
I just love that stereotype,
you know,

00:03:04.901 --> 00:03:07.567
that Jewish mothers are just so
bananas for their kids.

00:03:07.567 --> 00:03:08.767
-They really are.

00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:12.667
-"My Sharon, anywhere
you could fit a schmekel,

00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:13.701
there was Schmekel.

00:03:13.701 --> 00:03:15.200
-Okay!

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.834
-Top of her knee, a schmekel.
Her forehead, a schmekel.

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:23.133
You ever seen
a schmekel on an elbow?

00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:25.133
The left one, a doctor."

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:27.133
Too much?
-Oh, my gosh.

00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:28.968
-I thought I really
cleaned it up for this.

00:03:28.968 --> 00:03:32.701
-Oh, my gosh.
Unbelievable. Unbelievable.

00:03:32.701 --> 00:03:35.000
[ Applause ]
Applause break.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.400
Applause breaks through Zoom.

00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:40.801
You do a thing on your podcast

00:03:40.801 --> 00:03:44.434
where you give
advice to listeners.

00:03:44.434 --> 00:03:46.601
And I love that
part of your podcast.

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:48.834
Have you always
given advice to people?

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:49.868
Are you good at that?

00:03:49.868 --> 00:03:51.167
I mean,
have you always done that?

00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:53.534
-Yeah, I think so. I -- Yeah.

00:03:53.534 --> 00:03:54.868
I mean, I was just in New York.

00:03:54.868 --> 00:03:56.467
My best friend, Heidi,
lives in New York.

00:03:56.467 --> 00:03:59.300
You've met her, actually.
And she loves you.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.934
And she was like, "I've had
diarrhea for like three days.

00:04:03.934 --> 00:04:05.968
I don't know what is going on."

00:04:05.968 --> 00:04:07.767
And then -- You know when
you're in New York --

00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:09.234
She had me go into her backpack.

00:04:09.234 --> 00:04:11.434
She was like, "Get my water
out of my backpack."

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:13.767
-Yeah.
-And I get her water out.

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:17.367
She has, like, half a bag of
prunes in her backpack.

00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:19.968
I was like,
"Heidi, I solved the case.

00:04:19.968 --> 00:04:21.701
You're eating prunes."
-Yeah, exactly.

00:04:21.701 --> 00:04:23.701
-She's like,
"They're not prunes."

00:04:23.701 --> 00:04:27.868
And then she pointed to it.
She goes, "They're dried plums."

00:04:27.868 --> 00:04:30.567
I said, "What do you think
prunes are?"

00:04:30.901 --> 00:04:33.234
She's like,
"Are they dried plums?"

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:35.067
Yeah. She's a therapist,
by the way.

00:04:35.067 --> 00:04:36.968
I just want to point that out.

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:39.000
But I get it.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.367
Like, Big Prune
was probably like,

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:42.634
"We need to rebrand."

00:04:42.634 --> 00:04:43.801
-Big Prune.

00:04:43.801 --> 00:04:47.567
-People hear "prune,"
they think diarrhea.

00:04:47.567 --> 00:04:51.734
So, yeah,
call yourselves dried plums.

00:04:51.734 --> 00:04:53.767
I think they should be
called dried plums,

00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:55.801
but it should come with
a warning that says like,

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:58.367
"Warning -- these are prunes."

00:04:58.367 --> 00:05:00.400
-Prunes. Yes, of course.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.434
Big Prune is taking over.

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:04.767
It's so sad to see that happen.
-Big Prune.

00:05:04.767 --> 00:05:07.334
-I want to talk about
your movie, "Marry Me."

00:05:07.334 --> 00:05:10.067
The buzz is,
this is going to be --

00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:12.300
this is going to bring back
the rom-com.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.968
You're saving
the romantic comedy.

00:05:14.968 --> 00:05:19.167
-Jimmy, it's our movie.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:21.634
It's -- You have Jennifer Lopez.

00:05:21.634 --> 00:05:23.734
Anything Jennifer Lopez
in a movie, I'm there.

00:05:23.734 --> 00:05:25.133
Jennifer Lopez --
-Same.

00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:28.767
-I mean, how fun is it?
How -- She's just the greatest.

00:05:29.667 --> 00:05:32.300
-And she's --
By the way, like, you know,

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:35.033
you see her on Instagram, you
see her on TV, and in movies.

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:37.701
And they have filters,
and they do beauty work.

00:05:37.701 --> 00:05:40.567
And, yeah, she's beautiful,
but in person --

00:05:40.567 --> 00:05:42.467
-In real life. Oh, stunner.

00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:44.167
-She's a monster. No.

00:05:44.167 --> 00:05:46.033
She is so beautiful.
-She's so stunningly beautiful.

00:05:46.033 --> 00:05:50.300
-I mean, I look at her this
close and I'm just like, "How?

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.434
How is it possible?"

00:05:52.434 --> 00:05:54.334
-And she can sing and
she can dance

00:05:54.334 --> 00:05:56.000
and she can -- You do --

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.000
-Amazing singer, amazing dancer,
awesome actor.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.267
I love her movies.
-Yeah.

00:06:03.267 --> 00:06:05.734
-Anytime her movie is on,
I'm -- I stay there.

00:06:05.734 --> 00:06:08.067
She's just -- She's electric.

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:12.767
And then there's Owen Wilson,
who is another favorite,

00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:15.901
I mean, but so different,
such a different realm.

00:06:15.901 --> 00:06:20.501
Like, his writing, his comedy,
his beautiful acting.

00:06:20.501 --> 00:06:24.501
And they're so different, but
it's, like, electric, you know?

00:06:24.501 --> 00:06:25.734
-Yeah.
-It really is.

00:06:25.734 --> 00:06:27.601
It is bringing back the rom-com.

00:06:27.601 --> 00:06:29.133
I think you're so right.
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:29.133 --> 00:06:31.968
Here's Sarah Silverman,
Jennifer Lopez, and Owen Wilson

00:06:31.968 --> 00:06:33.133
in "Marry Me."

00:06:33.133 --> 00:06:34.133
Take a look at this.

00:06:34.133 --> 00:06:36.400
-Charlie! Come in!

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:38.767
-Oh, she's right there. Jesus.

00:06:38.767 --> 00:06:40.667
-Come over, guys.

00:06:40.667 --> 00:06:42.434
Charlie, Kat.
Kat, Charlie.

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:43.434
-Hi.
-How are you?

00:06:43.434 --> 00:06:45.501
Oh.
[ Chuckles ]

00:06:45.501 --> 00:06:47.334
-Okay.
[ Laughs ]

00:06:47.334 --> 00:06:48.734
-I'm Parker.

00:06:48.734 --> 00:06:50.734
I'm -- I am the one who brought
Charlie to the concert,

00:06:50.734 --> 00:06:52.267
so I'm basically --

00:06:52.267 --> 00:06:54.801
I guess I'm responsible
for this whole thing.

00:06:54.801 --> 00:06:56.534
-Hi, Parker.

00:06:56.534 --> 00:06:57.567
-Hey, Kat.

00:06:57.567 --> 00:06:59.067
-Hi.

00:06:59.067 --> 00:07:01.801
-Would you do me a favor,
uh, just two seconds

00:07:01.801 --> 00:07:03.567
and just say,
"Roxy, you really blew it"?

00:07:03.567 --> 00:07:06.467
-"Today Show" is a go
at 10:00 on Tuesday.

00:07:06.467 --> 00:07:07.901
-Sorry.
-They want, uh, uh...

00:07:07.901 --> 00:07:09.300
-Um, Charlie.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.501
-Charlie in studio
and Kat remote from London.

00:07:12.501 --> 00:07:14.334
-Kat.
-Excuse me one second.

00:07:14.334 --> 00:07:15.701
I just have to take...

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.667
-Bye, Kat.

00:07:17.667 --> 00:07:19.367
She's, like, a real person.
-What's going on?

00:07:19.367 --> 00:07:22.200
-Yeah! She's a real person.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:25.667
You blew it, Roxy!
Sarah Silverman, everybody!

00:07:25.667 --> 00:07:27.734
"Marry Me" is in theaters

00:07:27.734 --> 00:07:29.801
and streaming on Peacock
February 11th.

